<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Water Chenla</h1><p class="st-meta">707 CE – 802 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The southern, lowland half of divided Chenla in the 8th century, a fragmented Khmer realm of the Mekong plains and delta.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayavarman-i">Jayavarman I</a> around 681, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chenla">Chenla</a> lost its unity, and Chinese records from about 707 distinguish a southern Water Chenla, Shui Zhenla, from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/land-chenla">Land Chenla</a>. It covered the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river">Mekong</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tonle-sap">Tonle Sap</a> region and the delta, the old lands of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/funan">Funan</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Water <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chenla">Chenla</a> appears to have been divided among several rulers, including a queen Jayadevi known from an inscription of 713 who complained of hard times. Centres such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sambhupura">Sambhupura</a>, near Sambor on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river">Mekong</a>, and Aninditapura held local power. Its kings followed Shaivite cults and wrote in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-khmer">Old Khmer</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the late 8th century Javanese raids, possibly from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sailendra-dynasty">Sailendra</a> kingdom, struck the region. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayavarman-ii">Jayavarman II</a>, who had spent time in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, returned to unite the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> lands and in 802 proclaimed himself universal monarch, ending the division and founding the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/khmer-empire">Khmer Empire</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 707 CE – 802 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mainland Southeast Asia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:73.77%;width:1.89%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>707 CE – 802 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Jayadevi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism (Shaivism), Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Khmer</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/cambodia">Cambodia</a>, <a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Lower Chenla, Thủy Chân Lạp</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=754&amp;focus=water-chenla" aria-label="Water Chenla on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7545 1710 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Water Chenla lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7886 2003h0M7846 1933h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7865 1966h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7865 1966h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Cambodia. <a href="/atlas?year=754&amp;focus=water-chenla">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
